---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { GISCUS } from '../lib/giscus';

// The site's privacy notice (GDPR Art. 13 transparency). Deliberately concrete:
// it names every third party the browser actually contacts and every byte the
// site keeps on the reader's device, because for a static site that list is
// short enough to state exhaustively rather than hedge.
//
// KEEP IN SYNC — this page is prose ABOUT code, so it goes stale silently.
// Revisit it whenever any of these change:
//   • Comments.astro          the giscus load trigger + what it contacts
//   • Layout.astro            the localStorage theme key
//   • BlogSearch.tsx          search staying local (Pagefind ships an index, not queries)
//   • astro.config.mjs        any integration that adds a runtime third party
//   • any new embed, font host, analytics, or CDN — the "no analytics" claim
//     below is only true while it stays true.
const repoUrl = `https://github.com/${GISCUS.repo}`;
---

<Layout title="Privacy">
	<h1>Privacy</h1>
	<p class="lede">
		This is a personal site. It has no analytics, no advertising, no trackers,
		and sets no cookies of its own.
	</p>

	<h2>What the site stores on your device</h2>
	<p>
		One thing: your light/dark theme choice, under the key <code>theme</code> in
		your browser's local storage — and only if you press the theme toggle. It
		stays on your device, is never sent anywhere, and clearing your site data
		removes it. If you never touch the toggle, nothing is stored and the site
		follows your operating system's appearance setting.
	</p>
	<p>
		There are no cookies. Nothing here profiles you, follows you between visits,
		or tries to work out who you are.
	</p>

	<h2>Comments</h2>
	<p>
		Blog posts carry comments powered by <a href="https://giscus.app">giscus</a>,
		which stores the discussion in
		<a href={`${repoUrl}/discussions`}>GitHub Discussions</a> on this site's
		repository. The widget loads from <code>giscus.app</code> and its avatars
		from <code>githubusercontent.com</code>, so when it loads, those services
		receive your IP address, your browser's user-agent string, and the address
		of the post you're reading. That is inherent to loading anything from
		another server — it is not extra data collection by the widget.
	</p>
	<p>
		The comments load only when you scroll far enough down a post to reach them.
		Stop reading before the end and your browser never contacts giscus or GitHub
		at all. Reading comments is anonymous and needs no account.
	</p>
	<p>
		Signing in with GitHub is required only to <em>post</em> a comment or leave a
		reaction. If you do sign in, giscus keeps an access token in its own storage
		on <code>giscus.app</code> to hold your session — that is between you and
		giscus, and this site never sees it. Anything you post is a public GitHub
		Discussion comment under your GitHub account, governed by
		<a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement">
			GitHub's privacy statement</a>. To edit or delete a comment, use GitHub
		— the comment lives there, not here.
	</p>

	<h2>Search</h2>
	<p>
		Blog search runs entirely in your browser. The search index is downloaded as
		static files and queried locally; what you type is never sent to a server,
		here or anywhere else.
	</p>

	<h2>Hosting</h2>
	<p>
		The site is served by GitHub Pages as static files. Like any web server,
		GitHub's records requests — including IP addresses — for delivery and abuse
		prevention. That happens at the hosting layer, on every visit, and is
		covered by
		<a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement">
			GitHub's privacy statement</a>. It applies whether or not you go anywhere
		near the comments.
	</p>

	<h2>Everything else is local</h2>
	<p>
		Fonts, math typesetting, and the comment widget's stylesheet are all served
		from this site rather than a CDN. Apart from the comments described above,
		loading a page here contacts no third party.
	</p>

	<h2>Questions</h2>
	<p>
		Ask in <a href={`${repoUrl}/discussions`}>the discussions</a> or open an
		issue on <a href={repoUrl}>the repository</a>.
	</p>
</Layout>
